cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "trend-cycle-toolkit: US trend inflation, the output gap and trend output growth, re-estimated quarterly"
message: >-
  If you use the published series, cite this software record and the vintage you
  took the numbers from — the vintage stamp is in
  estimates/current/metadata.json and in the release tag. If you use one of the
  models, cite the paper whose method it implements: the five references below,
  mapped to models in README.md.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chan
    given-names: Joshua C. C.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3632-128X"
    affiliation: Purdue University
repository-code: "https://github.com/joshuaccchan/trend-cycle-toolkit"
abstract: >-
  Three US macroeconomic series — trend inflation, the output gap and trend
  output growth — estimated from Bayesian unobserved components models and
  re-estimated four times a year on current public data. Five models produce
  them: three trend inflation specifications, an unobserved components model
  with a second-order Markov trend that yields both an output gap and
  annualized trend output growth, and an unobserved components model with
  correlated trend and cycle errors and two breaks in trend output growth. The
  MATLAB library behind the estimates is the uc package under core/+uc, which
  holds the samplers, the state space draws, the data layer and the MCMC
  diagnostics as one tested set of functions. Every release writes the series,
  their credible bands and their diagnostics as CSV and freezes a copy of the
  whole release, so that revisions can be read off the record. Inputs come from
  FRED, the Philadelphia Fed's Survey of Professional Forecasters and the
  Federal Reserve Board's FRB/US package.
keywords:
  - trend inflation
  - output gap
  - trend output growth
  - trend-cycle decomposition
  - unobserved components models
  - Bayesian econometrics
  - state space models
  - stochastic volatility
  - Markov chain Monte Carlo
  - MATLAB
# Two licenses, set out in README.md. MIT covers all code (core/, estimates/);
# CC-BY-4.0 covers the estimates this repository publishes, which are a dataset.
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
# The five method sources — the papers whose models are implemented here — plus
# the companion repository. Each notes field names the model it corresponds to
# and the published package that implements the method, where one exists.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Why Has US Inflation Become Harder to Forecast?"
    authors:
      - family-names: Stock
        given-names: James H.
      - family-names: Watson
        given-names: Mark W.
    journal: "Journal of Money, Credit and Banking"
    year: 2007
    volume: 39
    issue: "s1"
    start: 3
    end: 33
    notes: "Method source for uc.models.ucsv_sw07, which produces a trend inflation series. The implementation follows chapter10/UCSV.m in the book repository, https://github.com/joshuaccchan/bayesian-macroeconometrics."
  - type: article
    title: "A New Model of Trend Inflation"
    authors:
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: Joshua C. C.
      - family-names: Koop
        given-names: Gary
      - family-names: Potter
        given-names: Simon M.
    journal: "Journal of Business and Economic Statistics"
    year: 2013
    volume: 31
    issue: 1
    start: 94
    end: 106
    notes: "Method source for uc.models.ar_trend_bound, which produces a trend inflation series. The published package that implements it is ARtrendbound.zip at https://joshuachan.org/code.html."
  - type: article
    title: "A New Model of Inflation, Trend Inflation, and Long-Run Inflation Expectations"
    authors:
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: Joshua C. C.
      - family-names: Clark
        given-names: Todd E.
      - family-names: Koop
        given-names: Gary
    journal: "Journal of Money, Credit and Banking"
    year: 2018
    volume: 50
    issue: 1
    start: 5
    end: 53
    notes: "Method source for uc.models.biuc_lrexp, which produces a trend inflation series from inflation together with long-run inflation expectations. The published package that implements it is trend_IE_code.zip at https://joshuachan.org/code.html. Cite the journal version rather than the 2015 Cleveland Fed working paper."
  - type: article
    title: "Reconciling Output Gaps: Unobserved Components Model and Hodrick-Prescott Filter"
    authors:
      - family-names: Grant
        given-names: Angelia L.
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: Joshua C. C.
    journal: "Journal of Economic Dynamics and Control"
    year: 2017
    volume: 75
    start: 114
    end: 121
    notes: "Method source for uc.models.uc_2m, which produces both the output gap and annualized trend output growth. The published package that implements it is output_gap_2M_code.zip at https://joshuachan.org/code.html."
  - type: article
    title: "A Bayesian Model Comparison for Trend-Cycle Decompositions of Output"
    authors:
      - family-names: Grant
        given-names: Angelia L.
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: Joshua C. C.
    journal: "Journal of Money, Credit and Banking"
    year: 2017
    volume: 49
    issue: "2-3"
    start: 525
    end: 552
    notes: "Method source for uc.models.ucur_break2, which produces an output gap from a model with correlated trend and cycle errors and two breaks in trend output growth. The published package that implements it is output_gap_code.zip at https://joshuachan.org/code.html."
  - type: software
    title: "bvar-toolkit: MATLAB code for large Bayesian VARs"
    authors:
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: Joshua C. C.
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3632-128X"
    repository-code: "https://github.com/joshuaccchan/bvar-toolkit"
    year: 2026
    notes: "Companion repository, built to the same protocol. Several core/+uc/+util and core/+uc/+sv functions are imported from it rather than re-derived, and the precision sampler package of Chan and Jeliazkov (2009) is archived there rather than duplicated here."
